{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":235407,"duration_api_ms":248686,"num_turns":23,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"63ddc2b3-9499-442c-bf58-ceaa2691de2e","total_cost_usd":1.3353606,"usage":{"input_tokens":1200,"cache_creation_input_tokens":66176,"cache_read_input_tokens":1049782,"output_tokens":7720,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":66176,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":1200,"outputTokens":7720,"cacheReadInputTokens":1049782,"cacheCreationInputTokens":66176,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.1374910000000003,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":19597,"outputTokens":6666,"cacheReadInputTokens":423976,"cacheCreationInputTokens":82036,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.1978696,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"In `route_dead_shrimp_to_detritus` (shrimp.rs:644-648), shrimp body mass is converted to detrital grams via `shrimp_body_detrital_mass_g` using species-specific `body_nitrogen_mg_per_g_wet_mass` and `body_carbon_mg_per_g_wet_mass`. However, the budget system reads `fine_detritus_g_total` N/C using the global `feed_n_to_c_ratio` (budget.rs:302-303). At default values these are algebraically identical (both produce a 0.16 N:C ratio), so conservation holds today. But if anyone customizes `body_nitrogen_mg_per_g_wet_mass` or `body_carbon_mg_per_g_wet_mass` to a ratio different from `feed_n_to_c_ratio`, the N and C deposited from dead shrimp will be measured differently by the budget than what was removed from the shrimp body pool, silently violating mass conservation. At minimum, add a `debug_assert!` in `route_dead_shrimp_to_detritus` that validates the implied body N:C ratio matches `feed_n_to_c_ratio` (or within tolerance), so the invariant is enforced until the detritus pool tracks its own composition. Alternatively, document the coupling constraint on the new body composition params.","rationale":"This is a latent conservation correctness bug. The whole point of this bead is mass conservation through death routing. Introducing tunable body composition params that can silently break conservation when they diverge from the feed ratio undermines the core invariant."},{"body":"In `fund_live_shrimp_biomass` (shrimp.rs:666-678), newly hatched/matured shrimp reserve cost is computed as `biomass_g * LIVE_BIOMASS_ORGANIC_FRACTION_G_PER_G` (fixed at 0.20). The death path uses species-specific `body_nitrogen_mg_per_g_wet_mass + body_carbon_mg_per_g_wet_mass` (sum = 200 mg/g = 0.20 at defaults) to compute body detrital mass. If body composition is customized, the sum may no longer equal `LIVE_BIOMASS_ORGANIC_FRACTION_G_PER_G * 1000`, creating an asymmetry between reserve funding and body accounting. Either derive the reserve cost from the same body composition params, or add a `debug_assert!` ensuring `(body_N + body_C) / 1000 == LIVE_BIOMASS_ORGANIC_FRACTION_G_PER_G`.","rationale":"Mirror of the first issue: the new tunable params interact with the hardcoded organic fraction constant used for birth/maturation funding. At defaults they agree; if customized they can diverge, creating imbalanced creation-vs-destruction accounting."},{"body":"The `with_budget_debug_env` function (budget_helpers.rs:151-163) uses `std::env::set_var` and `std::env::remove_var` which are unsound under Rust 2024 edition rules and deprecated since Rust 1.66 because they can cause data races with concurrent env reads on other threads. The mutex only serializes callers of this function, not arbitrary `std::env::var` reads elsewhere. For test-only code this is pragmatically acceptable, but add a comment acknowledging the soundness limitation and consider using a `Cell`/`AtomicBool` config flag instead of env vars for the debug toggle.","rationale":"This is a known Rust safety issue. While unlikely to cause real problems in test code, it's worth documenting since the env-var approach is technically unsound and may trigger warnings or errors in future Rust editions."}],"summary":"Reviewed all 12 changed files covering shrimp death→detritus routing, budget recording kind tracking, O2 balance explicit-stage enforcement, budget debug env locking, water change test isolation, and body composition param introduction. All tests pass. The core death routing logic is correct at default parameter values and the budget plumbing is clean. Three substantive concerns: (1) a latent conservation bug where the detritus pool's N:C is read using `feed_n_to_c_ratio` but shrimp body mass deposited uses species-specific composition — these match at defaults but can silently diverge if customized; (2) `fund_live_shrimp_biomass` uses the hardcoded organic fraction constant that can similarly diverge from customized body composition; (3) `std::env::set_var` usage in test infrastructure is technically unsound under Rust safety rules."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"dfb7884b-5823-47db-8475-6b4b41f809b4"}
